    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1035-a1">

                <pb facs="1007" n="841" xml:id="pg841"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure—Translation]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic"> Ministry of Foreign Affairs to the American Embassy
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Rome</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-05-02">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 2, 1917</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Referring to <hi rendition="#italic">notes verbales</hi> Nos. 1551 and 1571 of
                    the 17th and 27th of April last respectively, the Royal Ministry of Foreign
                    Affairs has the honor to inform the Embassy of the United States of America
                    that, according to a very recent communication of the Royal Ministry of War, the
                    military authorities of the Kingdom cannot consent to the release from military
                    service and the return to the United States of Guglielmo Andreaccio.</p>
                <p>It appears indeed after careful inquiry, that the young man in question has
                    resided in Italy for nine years, and is the son of a father who was once an
                    Italian citizen; he has therefore been duly entered on the list of <hi
                        rendition="#italic">leva</hi> of Ruoti, the commune of which his father was
                    a native.</p>
                <p>And whereas, at the time of his enrollment on the list on February 8, 1917 he did
                    not in any way claim foreign citizenship, he must be considered to have become
                    an Italian citizen according to Article 3 of Law No. 555 dated June 13, 1912,
                    and is therefore under the obligation of full military duties to the
                    Kingdom.</p>
            </div>
